#include <vector>
using namespace std;

vector<int> twoSum(vector<int>& a, int t) {
    int lo = 0, hi = (int)a.size() - 1;
    while (lo < hi) {
        int s = a[lo] + a[hi];
        if (s == t) return {lo + 1, hi + 1};
        if (s < t) ++lo; else --hi;
    }
    return {};
}
```
:::

**Reading it line by line.** `lo, hi = 0, len(a) - 1` plants one finger at each extreme. `while lo < hi` keeps the two fingers on *distinct* cells (never pair an element with itself, and stop the instant they cross). Each round reads `s = a[lo] + a[hi]` — the biggest sum reachable with this `lo` and the smallest reachable with this `hi` at once. `s == t` is the answer. `s < t` says even `lo`'s largest possible partner falls short, so `lo` can never be in any solution and `lo += 1` retires it; `s > t` is the mirror, and `hi -= 1` retires `hi`. Exactly one finger moves per step and neither ever moves back, so the loop runs at most $n-1$ times — the whole $O(n^2)$ triangle is *discarded*, not scanned.

:::

::: {.p-complexity}
**Check every pair** is $O(n^2)$ time, $O(1)$ space. **Fix one end, binary-search the partner** is $O(n\log n)$ time, $O(1)$ space — $n$ suffix searches of $\log n$ each. **Squeeze from both ends** is $O(n)$ time — each step moves $\mathit{lo}$ up or $\mathit{hi}$ down, so at most $n-1$ steps — and $O(1)$ space, which is *why* it beats the hash-map Two Sum of @sec-hash-p1-a here: sortedness is worth a whole $O(n)$ map. On an *unsorted* array that asks for original indices you would first sort a copy of $(\mathit{value}, \mathit{index})$ pairs, paying $O(n\log n)$ time and $O(n)$ space — at which point the hash map is simpler.
:::

::: {.p-edge}
- Exactly one solution is guaranteed, so the loop always returns; drop that guarantee (a general "count all pairs") and the same walk works but must not `return` on the first hit — @sec-tp-p5 builds the counting versions.
- Duplicates like $[1, 1]$ with $t = 2$: the ends are distinct indices, so $\mathit{lo}=0, \mathit{hi}=1$ returns $[1, 2]$ correctly. `while lo < hi` (strict) is what stops an element from pairing with itself: $[3, 4]$ with $t = 6$ must not return $(1, 1)$.
- Negative values and zero are fine — the correctness argument uses only the sorted order, never positivity.
:::

::: {.practice}
- [CF 2060C · Game of Mathletes](https://codeforces.com/problemset/problem/2060/C){rating="900"} — count how many disjoint pairs sum to $k$: sort, squeeze, and on a hit retire *both* ends.
- [LC 653 · Two Sum IV - Input is a BST](https://leetcode.com/problems/two-sum-iv-input-is-a-bst/){difficulty="Easy"} — "sorted" supplied by an in-order traversal; then squeeze.
- Solve LC 1 (@sec-hash-p1-a) and this problem side by side, and say which guarantee buys the $O(1)$ space here.
:::


[**From existence to extremum.** Two Sum II stops at the first pair that matches. Container With Most Water never stops — every step *improves* the best area seen, and the discard proof changes from "this end cannot complete the target" to "this end cannot pair better with anything inside."]{.flow-bridge}

### Problem: Container With Most Water {#sec-tp-p1-b}

::: {.p-statement}
Heights $h_0, \dots, h_{n-1}$ ($2 \le n \le 10^5$, $0 \le h_i \le 10^4$), one vertical line at each index. Pick two lines $i < j$ that with the axis form a container holding $\min(h_i, h_j) \cdot (j - i)$ units of water; return the **maximum** over all pairs. The container cannot be slanted, and $h_i$ is a height, not a bar you can fill behind.

Source: [LC 11 · Container With Most Water](https://leetcode.com/problems/container-with-most-water/){.solved difficulty="Medium"}
:::

::: {.p-intuition}
The area is a product of a *width* and the *shorter* wall. Starting at the widest possible span ($\mathit{lo}=0$, $\mathit{hi}=n-1$) and moving one pointer inward always shrinks the width by one, so the only way the area could still grow is if the height grows enough to pay for the lost width — and the height that can grow is the **shorter** wall's. Moving the taller wall inward keeps $\min$ the same (or worse) at a narrower span, a provably no-better choice; so you discard the shorter wall every step and never the wrong one.
:::

::: {.p-approach}
1. **Try all pairs.** Two loops maximise $\min(h_i, h_j)(j-i)$. $O(n^2)$ — at $n = 10^5$ that is $10^{10}$, hopeless.
2. **Try all pairs, but only wide ones.** Order the enumeration by *decreasing width* and stop the moment a whole strip of width $w$ cannot help: no pair narrower than $w$ can beat $w \cdot \max h$, so the same $O(n^2)$ worst case ends early on typical inputs — the honest middle step between the raw double loop and the squeeze.
3. **Move the shorter wall.** One pointer at each end; record the area, then advance whichever of $h[\mathit{lo}], h[\mathit{hi}]$ is smaller. $O(n)$.

**Invariant.** The optimum is never lost: when $h[\mathit{lo}] \le h[\mathit{hi}]$, every container using $\mathit{lo}$ at a width $\le \mathit{hi} - \mathit{lo}$ is capped by $h[\mathit{lo}]$ and narrower than the one just measured, so $\mathit{lo}$'s best is already recorded and it can be retired. The pruned enumeration keeps the weaker invariant "every *skipped* strip has an area ceiling $\le$ the recorded best," which is why early exit cannot discard the optimum.
:::

The buttons are in order. Each one keeps the previous solution and changes only what that form changes.

::: {.panel-tabset .solution-forms}

#### Try all pairs

::: {.form-next}
The definition, verbatim. $O(n^2)$ time, $O(1)$ space — $\binom{n}{2}$ exact areas, every one counted, and the maximum over all of them is the answer by construction. It is the oracle the squeeze is stress-tested against, and in an interview it is the correct thing to *state* before improving.

**The one formula, in plain words.** Two walls at indices $i < j$ hold water up to the *shorter* wall (the taller one spills over the low one), across the gap between them: $\text{area} = \min(h_i, h_j) \times (j - i)$. $\min$ picks the shorter height; $j - i$ is the width in index steps.

**Worked example.** $h = [1, 8, 6, 2, 5]$. Pair $(0, 4)$: $\min(1, 5) \times 4 = 4$. Pair $(1, 4)$: $\min(8, 5) \times 3 = 15$. Trying all ten pairs, $15$ is the largest — and it is the answer.
:::

