#include <vector>
#include <algorithm>
using namespace std;

vector<vector<int>> threeSum(vector<int>& a) {
    sort(a.begin(), a.end());
    int n = (int)a.size();
    vector<vector<int>> res;
    for (int i = 0; i + 2 < n; ++i) {
        if (a[i] > 0) break;
        if (i && a[i] == a[i - 1]) continue;
        int lo = i + 1, hi = n - 1;
        while (lo < hi) {
            int s = a[i] + a[lo] + a[hi];
            if (s < 0) ++lo;
            else if (s > 0) --hi;
            else {
                res.push_back({a[i], a[lo], a[hi]});
                int lv = a[lo], rv = a[hi];
                while (lo < hi && a[lo] == lv) ++lo;
                while (lo < hi && a[hi] == rv) --hi;
            }
        }
    }
    return res;
}
```
:::

**Reading it line by line.** `if nums[i] > 0: break` — because the array is sorted, once the pivot is positive all three numbers are positive and can no longer sum to $0$, so we stop. `if i and nums[i] == nums[i - 1]: continue` is the first dedup guard: re-running the squeeze on an identical pivot would re-emit the same triples, so we skip it. `lo, hi = i + 1, n - 1` runs the Two-Sum-II squeeze on the tail for target $-a[i]$: `s < 0` raises `lo` (need a bigger sum), `s > 0` lowers `hi`, `s == 0` emits the triple and advances *both*. The two inner `while` loops (`nums[lo] == nums[lo - 1]`, `nums[hi] == nums[hi + 1]`) are the second and third dedup guards — after a hit, hop over any repeated value at either end so the same triple is not produced twice from a different $(\mathit{lo}, \mathit{hi})$.

:::

::: {.p-complexity}
**Enumerate all triples** is $O(n^3)$ time; the set holds at most the number of distinct value triples. **Fix two, binary-search the third** is $O(n^2\log n)$ time — $\binom{n}{2}$ pairs, one $O(\log n)$ search each — plus the set. **Fix one, squeeze the rest** is $O(n^2)$ time — the pivot loop is $n$, each squeeze is a fresh $O(n)$ walk — and $O(1)$ auxiliary space beyond the output (the sort is in place; `sorted(nums)` in Python copies, so account for $O(n)$ there). Space for the answer itself can be $\Theta(n^2)$ triples on degenerate inputs like all zeros, which no algorithm can avoid.
:::

::: {.p-edge}
- All equal $[0,0,0,0]$: emits $[0,0,0]$ **once** — the $i$-skip and end-skips are doing that job; delete any one guard and the stress loop finds a duplicate immediately.
- Fewer than three elements: `range(n - 2)` is empty, answer `[]`.
- The early `break` on $a[i] > 0$ is a *sortedness* win (@sec-tp-ends); it only works because the array is sorted, and is the kind of micro-optimisation an interviewer likes to hear justified.
:::

::: {.practice}
- [LC 923 · 3Sum With Multiplicity](https://leetcode.com/problems/3sum-with-multiplicity/){difficulty="Medium"} — count *index* triples instead of listing value triples: the run batching of @sec-tp-fix (`dl * dr` and $\binom{m}{2}$) replaces the dedup skips.
- [LC 18 · 4Sum](https://leetcode.com/problems/4sum/){difficulty="Medium"} — fix *two*, squeeze the rest; the $O(n^2)$ becomes $O(n^3)$ and the dedup guards multiply.
- [LC 611 · Valid Triangle Number](https://leetcode.com/problems/valid-triangle-number/){difficulty="Medium"} — pivot + squeeze counting triples that satisfy $a + b > c$ instead of $= 0$.
:::


[**From "sum to zero" to "sum nearest to $t$."** 3Sum hunts an exact target and deduplicates hits. 3Sum Closest keeps the same fix-and-squeeze but the *best distance so far* replaces the halt: every step that improves it updates the answer, and the only early exit left is distance zero.]{.flow-bridge}

### Problem: 3Sum Closest {#sec-tp-p2-b}

::: {.p-statement}
An array $a$ of length $3 \le n \le 500$ with $-10^3 \le a_i \le 10^3$, and a $\mathit{target}$ in $[-10^4, 10^4]$. Find the three elements whose sum is **closest** to $\mathit{target}$ and return that sum. Exactly one solution exists.

Source: [LC 16 · 3Sum Closest](https://leetcode.com/problems/3sum-closest/){.solved difficulty="Medium"}
:::

::: {.p-intuition}
Same skeleton as 3Sum (@sec-tp-p2-a) — sort, fix $a[i]$, squeeze the tail — but the squeeze no longer hunts an exact target; it tracks the **best distance seen**. Because the tail is sorted, moving $\mathit{lo}$ up raises the sum and $\mathit{hi}$ down lowers it, so the sum sweeps monotonically toward $\mathit{target}$ and every visited triple is a candidate on the way. The one difference that bites: closeness is $|s - \mathit{target}|$, and two sums on opposite sides can be *equidistant* — compare distances, never the raw sums.
:::

::: {.p-approach}
1. **Enumerate triples.** Three loops, keep the sum of least distance. $O(n^3)$ — at $n = 500$ that is about $2\cdot10^7$ triples, borderline in C++ and far too slow in Python; it is the checker.
2. **Sort, fix two, bisect the nearest third.** Sort; per pair $(i, j)$ `bisect_left` for $t - a[i] - a[j]$ in the suffix and probe the insertion point's two neighbours, keeping the least-distance sum. $O(n^2 \log n)$.
3. **Sort, fix one, squeeze by distance.** Sort; for each pivot $i$, squeeze $[i{+}1, n)$, updating $\mathit{best}$ whenever $|s - \mathit{target}|$ improves; move $\mathit{lo}$ while $s < \mathit{target}$ and $\mathit{hi}$ otherwise; return early on an exact hit. $O(n^2)$.

**Invariant.** For a fixed pivot $i$, every pair that the squeeze retires is no closer than a sum it has already recorded. If $s = a_i + a_{\mathit{lo}} + a_{\mathit{hi}} < \mathit{target}$, then every $k < \mathit{hi}$ gives $a_i + a_{\mathit{lo}} + a_k \le s < \mathit{target}$, which is *further* from the target than $s$, so $\mathit{lo}$ can retire; the case $s > \mathit{target}$ retires $\mathit{hi}$ by the mirror argument. Across pivots, $\mathit{best}$ only improves.
:::

The buttons are in order. Each one keeps the previous solution and changes only what that form changes.

::: {.panel-tabset .solution-forms}

#### Enumerate all triples

::: {.form-next}
The direct reading: track the sum minimising $|s - \mathit{target}|$. $O(n^3)$, correct, and the tie behaviour is defined for free (strict `<` keeps the first-seen). Use it to stress form 2.

**"Closest", in plain words.** Instead of asking "does this triple sum to exactly the target?" we ask "how far is this triple's sum from the target?" — measured by $|s - \mathit{target}|$ (the distance, always $\ge 0$). We keep whichever sum we have seen so far has the *smallest* distance.

**Worked example.** nums $= [-1, 2, 1, -4]$, target $= 1$. The four triples give sums $2, -3, -4, -1$ with distances $1, 4, 5, 2$. The smallest distance is $1$, from the sum $2$ — so the answer is $2$.
:::

