## Further reading

- [CPH (Laaksonen), *Competitive Programmer's Handbook*](https://cses.fi/book/book.pdf) — ch. 8.1 "Two pointers method": the subarray-sum and 2SUM examples and the amortised argument that each pointer moves $O(n)$ times.
- [USACO Guide — Two Pointers](https://usaco.guide/silver/two-pointers) — a graded problem list for opposite-ends and same-direction pointers, with short solutions; a good next stop after this chapter.
- [Wikipedia — Merge algorithm](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Merge_algorithm) — the two-finger merge, its use in merge sort, $k$-way merging, and parallel merge.
- [Wikipedia — 3SUM](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/3SUM) — the quadratic algorithm of @sec-tp-fix, the 3SUM conjecture, the slightly subquadratic algorithms, and the problems known to be 3SUM-hard.
- [Wikipedia — Dutch national flag problem](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Dutch_national_flag_problem) — Dijkstra's three-pointer partition (exercise 18) and its use inside quicksort.
- [Wikipedia — Sort-merge join](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Sort-merge_join) — the merge of @sec-tp-merge as the join algorithm inside relational databases.
- [Wikipedia — Cheney's algorithm](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Cheney%27s_algorithm) — a copying garbage collector in which a scan pointer chases a free pointer through the new heap: the read/write fingers of A1 at systems scale.
